          "heading": "Integrated laboratory: Diagnose soil-water response to two contrasting storm sequences.",
          "content": "**Research task.** The same rainfall distributed over different soil, canopy and energy conditions Start from the attached `datasets/unit-02-synthetic.csv`. The records are deliberately fictional and provide no local environmental observations. The learner's deliverable is a short technical notebook, a table of measurements and conversions, a controlled comparison between two interpretations, and a one-page statement of residual uncertainty.\n\n**Phase A \u2014 conceptual diagram.** A high rainfall intensity does not guarantee high catchment runoff: infiltration capacity varies through an event, saturation patches can expand, and surface runoff may be disconnected from streams. Draw routing pathways through litter, upper soil, preferential macropores, lateral horizons and channels. Each observation has a spatial footprint; annotate which terms would require a point sample, area-average estimate or volumetric balance. For groundwater, show screened intervals and vertical datum. For rivers, mark the measurement section and route. For water chemistry, identify concentration, flow and analyte units. A diagram that omits the boundary often hides the missing flux that later produces an apparently anomalous numerical result.\n\n**Phase B \u2014 independent calculation.** A patch of surface water after a storm might be locally saturated, mechanically compacted or underlain by a shallow impermeable layer. A one-hour infiltration experiment does not distinguish them without additional depth observations. Use at least two independent calculation approaches where possible. Compare the result against a sanity bound: storage cannot be below a declared physically admissible minimum, chemical mixing fractions should lie between zero and one under the assumed ideal endmembers, and a probability cannot exceed unity. Round only after combining inputs; retain raw values and intermediate results in a reproducible notebook.\n\n**Phase C \u2014 evaluate the alternative.** Mass balance applies at each vertical boundary: throughfall, infiltration at surface, drainage below root zone and recharge at groundwater level are physically distinct quantities. Replace one assumption at a time. Include a small scenario table showing baseline, lower plausible input and upper plausible input, then a separate table for structural alternatives where equations themselves differ. Explain whether the compared outcomes cross a chosen decision threshold. If no real-world decision threshold has been justified, label the result descriptive only rather than declaring a policy winner.\n\n**Phase D \u2014 submit a traceable report.** Include the following in one coherent narrative: (a) research question and ethical scope, (b) boundary and major water or solute pathways, (c) original invented records, (d) exact equation and dimensional checks, (e) rival mechanisms and discriminating observations, (f) sensitivity or uncertainty table, and (g) a short conclusion distinguishing the measurement from its interpretation. State why the data cannot justify a real-world site decision.\n\n### Unit synthesis\n\nEvery lesson above contributes a different constraint to the integrated investigation. Cross-check a mass balance against a physically plausible flow path; cross-check a statistical summary against a documented measurement interval; and cross-check a model-derived conclusion against an independent observation wherever feasible. Report what evidence would overturn the preferred conclusion. The appropriate scientific endpoint is not a graph that looks convincing, but an interpretation that remains defensible when another researcher can inspect its data, assumptions and alternatives.",
