          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Integrated laboratory: Compare demand and supply policies under uncertain recharge.",
          "content": "**Research task.** A growing coastal district with dry-season withdrawals and uneven water service Start from the attached `datasets/unit-07-synthetic.csv`. The records are deliberately fictional and provide no local environmental observations. The learner's deliverable is a short technical notebook, a table of measurements and conversions, a controlled comparison between two interpretations, and a one-page statement of residual uncertainty.\n\n**Phase A \u2014 conceptual diagram.** Water availability depends on seasonality, water quality, cost, conveyance reliability, ecosystems and rights. A city may report a positive annual water balance while aquifers decline during the dry season or an upstream village has no safe supply. Each observation has a spatial footprint; annotate which terms would require a point sample, area-average estimate or volumetric balance. For groundwater, show screened intervals and vertical datum. For rivers, mark the measurement section and route. For water chemistry, identify concentration, flow and analyte units. A diagram that omits the boundary often hides the missing flux that later produces an apparently anomalous numerical result.\n\n**Phase B \u2014 independent calculation.** A drought index can signal atmospheric shortage before groundwater responds; the delay is a storage property rather than inconsistency. A coastal salinity trend might have several groundwater and surface-water explanations. Use at least two independent calculation approaches where possible. Compare the result against a sanity bound: storage cannot be below a declared physically admissible minimum, chemical mixing fractions should lie between zero and one under the assumed ideal endmembers, and a probability cannot exceed unity. Round only after combining inputs; retain raw values and intermediate results in a reproducible notebook.\n\n**Phase C \u2014 evaluate the alternative.** Planning under uncertainty should compare a baseline, a dry scenario and an intervention scenario with declared demands and ecological minimum flows. Calibrated models are not automatic guarantees when forcing changes. Replace one assumption at a time. Include a small scenario table showing baseline, lower plausible input and upper plausible input, then a separate table for structural alternatives where equations themselves differ. Explain whether the compared outcomes cross a chosen decision threshold. If no real-world decision threshold has been justified, label the result descriptive only rather than declaring a policy winner.\n\n**Phase D \u2014 submit a traceable report.** Include the following in one coherent narrative: (a) research question and ethical scope, (b) boundary and major water or solute pathways, (c) original invented records, (d) exact equation and dimensional checks, (e) rival mechanisms and discriminating observations, (f) sensitivity or uncertainty table, and (g) a short conclusion distinguishing the measurement from its interpretation. State why the data cannot justify a real-world site decision.\n\n### Unit synthesis\n\nEvery lesson above contributes a different constraint to the integrated investigation. Cross-check a mass balance against a physically plausible flow path; cross-check a statistical summary against a documented measurement interval; and cross-check a model-derived conclusion against an independent observation wherever feasible. Report what evidence would overturn the preferred conclusion. The appropriate scientific endpoint is not a graph that looks convincing, but an interpretation that remains defensible when another researcher can inspect its data, assumptions and alternatives.",
